      <section class="article-section" id="unpaid-weeks">
        <h2>What Unpaid Weeks Cost</h2>
        <p>The $124,800.00 headline is a 52-paid-week year. Unpaid time off, vacation without PTO, a seasonal gap, a slow January, comes straight off that year. The take-home cost looks bigger than the gross cost, because the weeks you lose are full-rate weeks.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="pay-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Paid weeks</th><th>Hours paid</th><th>Annual gross</th><th>Federal-only take-home</th><th>Take-home lost</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>52 (no unpaid time)</strong></td><td>2,080</td><td><strong>$124,800.00</strong></td><td><strong>$96,566.80</strong></td><td>—</td></tr>
              <tr><td>51 (1 unpaid week)</td><td>2,040</td><td>$122,400.00</td><td>$94,926.40</td><td>$1,640.40</td></tr>
              <tr><td>50 (2 unpaid weeks)</td><td>2,000</td><td>$120,000.00</td><td>$93,250.00</td><td>$3,316.80</td></tr>
              <tr><td>48 (4 unpaid weeks)</td><td>1,920</td><td>$115,200.00</td><td>$89,873.20</td><td>$6,693.60</td></tr>
              <tr><td>39 (13 unpaid weeks)</td><td>1,560</td><td>$93,600.00</td><td>$74,677.60</td><td>$21,889.20</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Start with one week: $2,400.00 of gross gone, $1,640.40 of take-home gone, which is more than a $1-an-hour raise is worth ($1,421.68). That is the honest way to weigh a job with unpaid gaps against one with paid time off. The rung drop that started at $56 is now a full step wider. One unpaid week at $60 leaves $94,926.40, which falls $218.72 short of a full-time $59-an-hour year, while at $59 the same week fell only $196.98 short of a full-time $58-an-hour year. Two unpaid weeks cost $4,800.00 gross and $3,316.80 of take-home, landing $450.24 short of a full-time $58-an-hour year, though still $1,013.04 ahead of a full-time $57-an-hour year. Four unpaid weeks cost $9,600.00 gross and $6,693.60 of take-home, landing between a full-time $55-an-hour year and a $56-an-hour year, $562.80 ahead of $55 and $900.48 short of $56, yet the year still beats a $100,000 salary by $15,200.00 gross and $10,693.20 of take-home. The thirteen-week row is the real flip on this page: $74,677.60 of take-home trails a $100,000 salary by $4,502.40, while at $55, thirteen unpaid weeks still beat it by $70.35, so the quarter-year line moved four rungs ago and has not come back. Two ties close the section: one unpaid week at $60 still beats a full-time $58-an-hour year by $1,226.16, so the first unpaid week only drops you one rung, and thirteen unpaid weeks land on exactly the same $93,600.00 gross as a 30-hour week worked all year, which is why the schedules table and this table agree to the cent. Our <a href="/articles/how-much-is-59-an-hour-a-year/">$59-an-hour guide</a> and <a href="/articles/how-much-is-58-an-hour-a-year/">$58-an-hour guide</a> price the same unpaid-week steps at their rates, so the three guides read as one ladder.</p>
      </section>
